course: dictionary
lesson: 11749
title: Bailiwick
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A bailiwick is a noun, a naming word for a place or area. In everyday talk, it is a person's own area of skill, interest or responsibility. When you read «Cakes are Dad's bailiwick, and he bakes one every Sunday», the words «bakes one every Sunday» are the clues. Dad is good at cakes and is in charge of making them.
explain: The second clue is the phrase «outside my bailiwick». Look at the words around it: «Please don't ask me about trains; that is outside my bailiwick.» If something is outside your bailiwick, it is not your area, so you do not know much about it.
explain: Bailiwick also has a law meaning. Long ago, a bailiff (an officer who kept order for a ruler) had an area to look after, and that area was called a bailiwick. Today, Jersey and Guernsey in the Channel Islands are each called a Bailiwick. Context tells you which meaning is in use: a place name, or a person's skill and job.
fact: Bailiwick is a noun, and its plural is bailiwicks.
fact: «Bailiwick» means a person's area of skill, interest or responsibility.
fact: «Outside my bailiwick» means not something I know about or am in charge of.
fact: In law, a bailiwick was the area ruled by a bailiff.
fact: Jersey and Guernsey, in the Channel Islands, are each called a Bailiwick today.
exercise: «Cakes are Dad's bailiwick, and he bakes one every Sunday.» What does bailiwick mean here? | A Sunday holiday | A cake shop in the town | *Something he is in charge of and good at | A type of oven
exercise: «Please don't ask me about trains; that is outside my bailiwick.» What does outside my bailiwick suggest? | *It is not something I know about or am in charge of | It is a long way from home | It is a train station near my house | It is something I love most
exercise: Jersey and Guernsey are each called a Bailiwick. Which kind of word is bailiwick here? | A number | *A place name, meaning an area ruled by a bailiff | A verb, meaning to bake | A colour
test: Read the sentence: «Fixing the computer is Mia's bailiwick.» Who is good at fixing computers? | *Mia | Her teacher | The bailiff | Nobody at all
test: Swap your meaning into «Maths is my bailiwick.» Does «area of skill» still make sense? | No, because it is always a town | No, maths is a type of cake | Yes, it means a lake | *Yes, maths is my area of skill
exam: «Chess is Ravi's bailiwick, but football is outside his bailiwick.» What does this tell you? | Chess and football are the same thing | Ravi lives in Jersey | Ravi plays football better than chess | *Ravi is good at chess and does not know much about football
